<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abylai Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1711–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ablai Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abylai Khan was a Kazakh ruler of the Middle Juz and a hero of the wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, during which he was for a time held prisoner by them. After the Qing destroyed the Dzungar state in the 1750s, he balanced skilfully between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, acknowledging both, and in 1771 he was proclaimed khan of all three Kazakh juzes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>. He is regarded as one of the most important figures in Kazakh history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abylai-khan.jpg" alt="Abylai Khan" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kazpost · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2000_Stamp_of_Kazakhstan_-_Abylai_Khan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Waliullah Abu'l-Mansur, Abylai belonged to the senior branch of the descendants of Janybek Khan, the 15th-century founder of the Kazakh state. His father was Korkem Wali Sultan. He spent his childhood and part of his youth in exile, and after losing his father to political rivals at the age of thirteen he worked as a herdsman. He was called Sabalak, the beggar, a name he kept so that his father's rivals would not notice a descendant of Janybek. At fifteen he began to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> in the Kazakh militia during the Kazakh-Dzungar War of 1723-1729. Shoqan Walikhanov wrote that the future sultan won universal attention, first as an ordinary warrior, for his courage and strategic insight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1741 an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> army under Septen invaded the Middle Jüz. Abylai, commanding a reconnaissance detachment of 200 men, charged into the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> force and was captured with his companions. He was held at the headquarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a>, where he learned the Oirat language and script and met the Khoit noyan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amursana">Amursana</a>. During negotiations he was at times shackled to pressure Kazakh envoys. By the late 1740s the Dzungar Khanate was in decline, and in 1751 the defeated noyan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawachi">Dawachi</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. When the Dzungar khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lama-dorji">Lama Dorji</a> demanded the extradition of Dawachi and Amursana, Abylai replied that they had come of their own accord, and the Kazakh camps moved north, closer to the Russian frontier.</p><p>In 1755 a Qing army of about 200,000 conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a>. Amursana, left with about two hundred warriors, took refuge in Abylai's camps. The Qing emperor then ordered his commanders either to persuade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> to withdraw or to secure recognition of his suzerainty by force. Abylai refused to comply and marched against the Qing forces with the Middle Jüz militia. He also campaigned against the Kyrgyz and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> ruler Irdana, and in 1779 led an army of about 12,000 against the Kyrgyz, taking prominent families hostage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the spring of 1780 Abylai moved to the area near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. In the autumn he fell seriously ill and died shortly afterwards, near Tashkent; the exact date is unknown. He was buried with great honours in the Mausoleum of Khoja Ahmed Yasawi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>, and more than 5,000 people attended the funeral feast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> raised Wali Sultan to the position of khan.</p><p>Abylai sought to unite the fragmented Kazakh tribes and was among the first to recognise the suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> voluntarily. Assessments of him vary widely: some portray him as a cunning barbarian, others admire his political and military work, and Soviet and Chinese publications have often treated him in a politicised way. He is important in the historiography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ablai Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its long struggle with the Dzungars, above all the 'Years of the Great Disaster' (Aqtaban shubyryndy) after 1723, became central to Kazakh national history, as did <b>Abylai Khan</b> and the batyrs who fought beside him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.3,68.27&amp;year=1756" aria-label="Turkistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abylai Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5513 3854h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5513 3854h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.3,68.27&amp;year=1756">What was here in 1756? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
